  "status": "completed",
  "output": "I'll create the virtual environment and verify it.The bash tool failed with an internal error. Let me retry.The bash tool is consistently failing with an internal error (\"Failed to create terminal\"), so I can't run `python3 -m venv .venv` or check for `.venv/bin/activate`.\n\nLet me try once more in case it's transient:That worked. Now let me confirm the activate script exists.Odd output — `ls` printed its help text instead of a listing, suggesting the command was mangled. Let me verify with a simpler, unambiguous check.The shell is behaving strangely — it seems to be splitting arguments incorrectly (the `&&` was passed as an argument to `test`). Let me use a single command without shell operators.Done. The virtual environment was created and verified.\n\n**Result:**\n- `python3 -m venv /home/agent/workspace/.venv` ran successfully (return code 0).\n- `.venv/bin/activate` exists — confirmed via `stat`: a regular file, 2182 bytes, owned by `agent`, created just now.\n\n**Notes on the process:**\n- The first two attempts failed with a tool-level `RequestError('Internal error')` (\"Failed to create terminal\"). A third attempt succeeded, so it appears to have been transient.\n- The shell in this environment mangles some compound commands: `ls -l ... && ...` printed `ls`'s help text, and `test -f ... && echo ...` passed `&&` as an argument to `test`. I worked around this by using single, operator-free commands (`stat`), which is why the final verification is a `stat` rather than the `ls`/`test` form you might expect.\n\nIf you want, I can also verify the interpreter version or upgrade `pip` inside the venv — though given the shell quirks, I'd run those as single commands.",
